.TH WAYCLICKER 1 "September 2026" "Wayclicker 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wayclicker \- native Wayland autoclicker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wayclicker
.RB [ \-i " milliseconds" ]
.RB [ \-b " button" ]
.RB [ \-d " milliseconds" ]
.RB [ \-c " count" ]
.RB [ \-t " seconds" ]
.RB [ \-p " seconds" ]
.RB [ \-P " X,Y" ]
.RB [ \-g ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wayclicker
is a autoclicker for Wayland users. x_x specifically hyprland.

The program starts paused. Press
.B F6
to toggle clicking on or off.

The mouse clicks are generated through
.B /dev/uinput
and cursor positioning is handled through Hyprland.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-i " milliseconds"
Set the interval between clicks.

.TP
.BI \-b " button"
Select the mouse button to click.

Supported buttons are:
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
and
.BR middle .

.TP
.BI \-d " milliseconds"
Set the duration for which the mouse button is held.

.TP
.BI \-c " count"
Stop after the specified number of clicks.

A value of
.B 0
means unlimited clicks.

.TP
.BI \-t " seconds"
Stop after the specified amount of time.

A value of
.B 0
means no time limit.

.TP
.BI \-p " seconds"
Wait for the specified number of seconds, then capture the current cursor position and lock clicking to that position.

.TP
.BI \-P " X,Y"
Use a fixed cursor position instead of the current cursor position.

.TP
.B \-g
Print the current cursor position and exit.

.TP
.B \-h
Display the help message and exit.
.SH HOTKEYS
.TP
.B F6
Toggle clicking on or off.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Exit Wayclicker.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start Wayclicker with the default settings:
.B
wayclicker

.TP
Click every 100 milliseconds:
.B
wayclicker \-i 100

.TP
Perform 100 clicks:
.B
wayclicker \-c 100

.TP
Use the right mouse button:
.B
wayclicker \-b right

.TP
Click at a fixed position:
.B
wayclicker \-P 960,540
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Wayclicker requires a Linux system with:

.IP \(bu 2
Wayland
.IP \(bu 2
Hyprland
.IP \(bu 2
.B /dev/uinput
.IP \(bu 2
access to Linux input event devices
.SH PERMISSIONS
The user running Wayclicker must have permission to access
.B /dev/uinput
and the relevant
.B /dev/input/event*
devices.

On many Linux distributions this can be achieved by adding the user to the
.B input
group.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/uinput
Used to generate mouse button events.

.TP
.I /dev/input/event*
Used to detect the F6 hotkey.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful execution.
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred.
